<td>Food, dairy, and pharmaceutical washdown areas</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<h3>How to Choose Bracket Hardware</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Changeover frequency.</strong> Fixed or bolted brackets are fine when the product never changes. Knob, cam-lever, or indexed hardware pays off as changeovers increase.</li>
<li><strong>Repeatability.</strong> Hand-set clamps depend on the operator. Scales, marked rods, detents, or a crank that moves the whole rail make settings repeatable from shift to shift.</li>
<li><strong>Bracket spacing and stiffness.</strong> Product pressure bends a rail between brackets. Heavier products, accumulation, and long spans need closer bracket spacing or a stiffer rail backbone.</li>
<li><strong>Height adjustment.</strong> Products of different heights may need the rail contact line moved up or down as well as in or out. Rod-and-clamp systems handle both.</li>
<li><strong>Sanitation.</strong> In washdown areas, use stainless hardware with open shapes, few threads exposed, and no hollow tubes that trap water. See <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary conveyor design</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Fit to the frame.</strong> Many OEM frames use proprietary mounting slots. <a href="https://www.dornerconveyors.com/products/2200-series/2200-precision-move-profiles" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Dorner's 2200 Series side guide profiles</a>, for example, include fixed aluminum sides alongside adjustable, tool-less adjustable, outboard, and horizontal adjustable guiding options for the same frame. Confirm that aftermarket brackets match the frame slot before ordering.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="gap-heading">Guide Rail Gap Settings</h2>
<p>The gap between guide rails and the product affects transport reliability, product damage, and downstream operation accuracy. The settings below are relative starting points. Confirm them by testing.</p>
<h3>Standard Transport Gap</h3>
<p>For general transport, set a small running clearance on each side of the product. This allows smooth flow while preventing excessive lateral movement. Products should be able to pass without rubbing but should not be able to rotate significantly.</p>
<h3>Precision Positioning Gap</h3>
<p>Before labelers, printers, <a href="/conveyor-functions/inspection-detection/">vision inspection systems</a>, or <a href="/conveyor-functions/indexing-conveyors/">indexing stations</a>, tighten the clearance below the transport setting. This ensures consistent product position for accurate label placement or image capture. Use low-friction materials (UHMW or acetal) to prevent product scuffing at tight gaps.</p>
<h3>Accumulation Zone Gap</h3>
<p>In <a href="/conveyor-types/accumulation-conveyors/">accumulation zones</a> where products queue under back-pressure, widen the clearance beyond the transport setting. Tighter gaps can cause products to jam, wedge, or tip when pressure builds. The exact gap depends on product stiffness and accumulation pressure.</p>
<h3>Variable Product Gap</h3>
<p>For products with significant dimensional variation (such as natural products, pouches, or bags), set the gap to accommodate the largest expected dimension plus a running clearance. Consider spring-loaded guides that self-adjust to product width. For products that expand during transport (such as warm products cooling), account for the expansion in the initial gap setting.</p>
<h4>Gap Setting Checklist</h4>
<ul>
<li>Measure the actual product width at multiple points (top, middle, bottom)</li>
<li>Account for product variation within the same SKU</li>
<li>Test with the largest and smallest products in the range</li>
<li>Verify that products do not rub or scuff at normal conveyor speed</li>
<li>Confirm that products cannot rotate more than a few degrees</li>
<li>Check for jamming under accumulation back-pressure</li>
<li>Document the gap setting for each SKU for repeatable changeover</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="applications-heading">Guide Rail Applications</h2>
<h3>Packaging Line Integration</h3>
<p>Guide rails are essential throughout <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/">packaging lines</a>. On <a href="/conveyor-functions/infeed-outfeed/">infeed conveyors</a>, guides funnel products into single-file for filling or wrapping machines. Between operations, guides maintain product orientation established by upstream equipment. Before <a href="/industries/packaging-line-conveyors/case/">case packers</a>, lane dividers create the product patterns required for case loading.</p>
<h3>Food and Beverage</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/food-conveyors/">Food and beverage operations</a> require <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/sanitary/">sanitary guide rails</a> that withstand daily washdown. Stainless steel rails with replaceable UHMW wear strips are standard. <a href="/conveyor-types/tabletop-conveyors/">Tabletop chain conveyors</a> for bottles and cans use curved guide rails that follow the chain path through turns and transfers. <a href="/industries/food-conveyors/bakery/">Bakery lines</a> use low-profile guides that do not interfere with overhead depositors or decorators.</p>
<h3>Pharmaceutical and Medical</h3>
<p><a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/">Pharmaceutical conveyors</a> require precision guide rails for accurate product positioning before labeling, serialization, and vision inspection. <a href="/industries/pharmaceutical-conveyors/cleanroom/">Cleanroom applications</a> use non-shedding materials and smooth surfaces that do not trap particles. Ask suppliers about particle shedding for each rail material before specifying it for a cleanroom.</p>
<h3>Distribution and Fulfillment</h3>
<p>Distribution center conveyors use guide rails to contain cartons and totes on <a href="/conveyor-types/roller-conveyors/">roller conveyors</a> and prevent tipover during <a href="/conveyor-types/diverting-merging/">sortation and merging</a>. Adjustable guides accommodate the wide range of package sizes in e-commerce fulfillment. <a href="/conveyor-types/incline-conveyors/">Incline conveyors</a> use top hold-down rails in addition to side guides to prevent product rollback.</p>
<h2 id="sourcing-heading">Guide Rail Sourcing</h2>
<p>Guide rails are available from three primary sources: the conveyor manufacturer (OEM), conveyor component specialists, and plastic/metal distributors for raw material fabrication.</p>
<p><strong>Conveyor manufacturers</strong> sell complete guide rail systems designed to fit their conveyor frames. These include mounting brackets, adjustment hardware, and rail profiles matched to the conveyor. Advantages: guaranteed fit, single-source warranty. Disadvantage: may cost more than aftermarket options.</p>
<p><strong>Aftermarket component suppliers</strong> sell guide rail profiles, brackets, and adjustment hardware compatible with multiple conveyor brands, often with a wider selection of materials and adjustment options than the conveyor OEM. Local industrial distributors typically stock standard guide rail components.</p>
<p><strong>Raw material fabrication</strong> is common for simple UHMW guide rails. UHMW sheet and bar stock from plastics distributors can be cut to size with standard woodworking tools. Custom stainless steel rails are fabricated by sheet metal shops.</p>
<p>For replacement guide rails, measure the existing rail profile, mounting hole pattern, and bracket dimensions. Many guide rail profiles are standard across manufacturers, but proprietary mounting systems may require OEM components.</p>
